1
00:00:01,600 --> 00:00:11,180
Many wells flow into one facility, and their production is measured together. So how do we know what each well produced? The answer is back allocation.

2
00:00:13,680 --> 00:00:24,779
Fluids enter each well from the reservoir and reach the surface by natural flow or artificial lift. The wellhead stream is a mixture of oil, water and gas.

3
00:00:25,279 --> 00:00:34,579
Flowlines carry production from wells A, B and C to a gathering manifold. There the streams mix and go on to separation and processing.

4
00:00:35,079 --> 00:00:45,982
After mixing, a conventional meter sees only the combined stream. The colours here show origin for you, the viewer. The meter cannot tell the sources apart.

5
00:00:47,882 --> 00:00:58,273
Measuring every phase at every well, all the time, is rarely possible. Wells are tested periodically, metered with multiphase meters, or estimated with models.

6
00:00:58,773 --> 00:01:05,573
Combined, processed streams are measured much more reliably, but they already contain commingled production.

7
00:01:06,073 --> 00:01:15,547
Back allocation works in the opposite direction to the flow. It assigns the measured totals back to facilities, fields and individual wells.

8
00:01:16,047 --> 00:01:25,177
The result is a reconciled estimate for every source. It remains a calculated value with uncertainty, even when the total is measured accurately.

9
00:01:25,677 --> 00:01:38,900
It provides daily and monthly well production, supports water-cut and gas–oil-ratio analysis, guides well testing, and builds the production history for reservoir management.

10
00:01:40,800 --> 00:01:47,033
Three main quantities are allocated: oil, gas and water, each on a defined accounting basis.

11
00:01:47,533 --> 00:01:57,621
Where the process requires it, condensate and natural gas liquids, individual components, or gas energy are allocated as well.

12
00:01:58,121 --> 00:02:06,469
Oil, gas and water should not automatically share one allocation factor. Their meters, routes, inventories and rules are different.

13
00:02:08,369 --> 00:02:16,390
Start by drawing the boundary of the balance area: a well group, a platform, a facility or a whole field.

14
00:02:16,890 --> 00:02:26,142
Every stream crossing the boundary is an input or an output: production, imports, export, fuel, flare, disposal and injection.

15
00:02:26,642 --> 00:02:35,861
Streams that stay inside are internal. Lift gas circulating within the boundary does not become new production each time it goes round.

16
00:02:36,361 --> 00:02:46,205
Inside the boundary, the calculation network is built from nodes, such as wells, manifolds, separators, tanks and meters, and the connections between them.

17
00:02:46,705 --> 00:02:58,780
Every connection has an effective period. If a well flows to facility one before noon and to facility two afterwards, its daily quantity is split by routing time.

18
00:03:00,680 --> 00:03:10,502
Next, define the accounting period: the start and end of the production day, the time zone and the closing rules. A production day does not have to begin at midnight.

19
00:03:11,002 --> 00:03:23,937
A rate is a quantity per unit time. A period volume is what accumulates. A cumulative meter reading becomes a period quantity by subtracting the opening reading from the closing one.

20
00:03:24,437 --> 00:03:35,838
Operating time matters. In our example, well A runs for all 24 hours, well B for 18 hours, and well C for only 12.

21
00:03:36,338 --> 00:03:48,598
Flow also takes time to arrive, and pipelines and vessels can store or release fluid. So today’s export cannot simply be assigned to today’s well inflow.

22
00:03:50,498 --> 00:04:02,513
The calculation needs data: well rates and tests, operating time and routing, shared meters, inventories, fluid quality, and special streams such as gas lift, fuel and flare.

23
00:04:03,013 --> 00:04:13,845
The data come from automated systems, process historians, laboratories and operating logs. Every value keeps its source, timestamp and quality status.

24
00:04:14,345 --> 00:04:23,411
Before any calculation, the data are validated: missing values, duplicates, impossible readings, wrong units, and timestamps outside the period.

25
00:04:23,911 --> 00:04:36,022
Missing data do not mean zero production. A genuine zero is backed by shutdown information or a valid measurement. An unknown value is marked as missing or estimated.

26
00:04:37,922 --> 00:04:49,672
To learn what one well produces, it is routed on its own to a test separator. Oil, gas and water are separated and measured, along with pressure, temperature and test duration.

27
00:04:50,172 --> 00:04:57,452
The first data after switching are transient. Only the stable part of a validated test is used.

28
00:04:57,952 --> 00:05:05,823
A test is a snapshot of a rate under particular conditions. After a choke or pump change, it may no longer be representative.

29
00:05:06,323 --> 00:05:17,993
There are other sources too. A multiphase flow meter estimates phase rates without separating the stream. A virtual flow meter calculates them from pressures, temperatures and a well model.

30
00:05:18,493 --> 00:05:28,408
The priority between these sources is defined in advance. And tested production returns to the main process, so it must not be counted twice.

31
00:05:30,308 --> 00:05:39,190
Volumes measured at different pressures and temperatures cannot simply be added. Gas volume is especially sensitive to conditions.

32
00:05:39,690 --> 00:05:49,685
So every phase is converted to defined reference conditions: a stated temperature, an absolute pressure, and a dry or wet gas convention.

33
00:05:50,185 --> 00:06:01,216
Processing changes the phases too. In the separator, gas comes out of the oil, so stabilised tank oil is less than separator oil. This shrinkage is not a leak.

34
00:06:01,716 --> 00:06:11,678
Laboratory factors or a PVT model make these conversions. Mass is conserved through phase changes, even when volumes are not.

35
00:06:13,578 --> 00:06:24,341
Now the calculation. For a well with a constant rate, the preliminary volume is the daily rate multiplied by the operating hours and divided by 24.

36
00:06:24,841 --> 00:06:37,902
Well A: 60 m³ per day for 24 hours gives 60 m³ of liquid. With a 20% water cut, that is 48 m³ of oil and 12 m³ of water.

37
00:06:38,402 --> 00:06:49,234
Well B: 40 m³ per day, but only 18 hours: 30 m³ of liquid. Half of it is water, so 15 m³ of oil and 15 m³ of water.

38
00:06:49,734 --> 00:07:02,018
Well C: 20 m³ per day for 12 hours gives 10 m³. At a 75% water cut: 2.5 m³ of oil and 7.5 m³ of water.

39
00:07:02,518 --> 00:07:12,387
Together: 100 m³ of liquid, of which 65.5 m³ is preliminary oil. These are first estimates, not final allocated results.

40
00:07:14,287 --> 00:07:23,146
The measured side comes from a material balance. Production plus external inputs equals outputs plus the change in inventory.

41
00:07:23,646 --> 00:07:34,491
So production is not the same as export. If oil accumulates in storage, export is lower than production. If stock is drawn down, export is higher.

42
00:07:34,991 --> 00:07:46,056
In our example, the export meter records 64.05 m³ for the day. The tank inventory rises from 20 to 28 m³.

43
00:07:46,556 --> 00:07:59,722
So the control quantity of produced oil is 64.05 plus 28 minus 20: that is 72.05 m³. This is the total we allocate.

44
00:08:01,622 --> 00:08:10,516
Now we work backwards. The control quantity is distributed to the wells in proportion to their preliminary estimates.

45
00:08:11,016 --> 00:08:21,104
Each well’s share is its preliminary volume divided by the total: about 73% for A, 23% for B and 4% for C.

46
00:08:21,604 --> 00:08:33,863
Equivalently, one reconciliation factor K is the control quantity divided by the preliminary total: 72.05 over 65.5 equals 1.10.

47
00:08:34,363 --> 00:08:48,234
Well A is allocated 52.80 m³, well B 16.50 and well C 2.75. Their sum equals the control quantity exactly.

48
00:08:48,734 --> 00:08:59,613
The 10% increase does not mean more oil was produced. The estimate was reconciled, and the factor alone does not say which well was in error.

49
00:09:01,513 --> 00:09:14,540
Water is allocated separately. An independent water balance gives 31.05 m³, against a preliminary 34.5: a factor of 0.90.

50
00:09:15,040 --> 00:09:23,619
Allocated water becomes 10.80 m³ for A, 13.50 for B and 6.75 for C.

51
00:09:24,119 --> 00:09:37,867
Gas needs extra care. Gas leaving a gas-lifted well contains reservoir gas and returning lift gas. Without telling them apart, the same gas would be counted as new production again and again.

52
00:09:38,367 --> 00:09:48,234
Subtracting the returning lift gas leaves 2400, 900 and 200 Sm³ of reservoir gas: 3500 in total.

53
00:09:48,734 --> 00:10:00,251
The gas balance adds every outlet, sales, fuel, flare and injection, and subtracts the external lift gas: 4200 Sm³.

54
00:10:00,751 --> 00:10:19,072
The gas factor is 1.20, giving 2880, 1080 and 240 Sm³. Flared and fuel gas were produced too: they are uses of gas, not gas that never existed.

55
00:10:20,972 --> 00:10:31,699
In a large network, allocation moves through levels: first from the overall total to facilities or fields, then to well groups, and then to wells.

56
00:10:32,199 --> 00:10:42,741
A valid intermediate meter constrains the wells downstream of it. One universal factor cannot simply be applied across facilities with different measurement arrangements.

57
00:10:43,241 --> 00:10:53,890
Other methods exist. The difference method subtracts reliably measured contributions and assigns what is left. But a small remainder of two large numbers is very uncertain.

58
00:10:54,390 --> 00:11:07,428
Component and energy allocation follow composition and heating value. Statistical reconciliation adjusts each measurement according to its uncertainty, while keeping the balances closed.

59
00:11:09,328 --> 00:11:21,924
Before reconciling, look at the residual: 72.05 minus 65.5 is 6.55 m³, or 10% of the preliminary total.

60
00:11:22,424 --> 00:11:38,086
Possible causes include outdated tests, wrong runtime, meter or water-cut errors, inventory errors, wrong routing, missing streams or mismatched periods. A residual alone does not prove a leak.

61
00:11:38,586 --> 00:11:49,896
Proportional allocation always closes the total. That confirms the arithmetic, not the accuracy of any single well. Imbalances beyond the acceptance criteria are investigated.

62
00:11:50,396 --> 00:12:05,882
Exceptions follow rules. A well shut in for the whole period gets zero: it earns no share just for being in the master data. And a missing estimate must never silently become zero.

63
00:12:06,382 --> 00:12:17,934
Deferment is different again: production that was possible but did not happen. That oil never passed through the facility, so it stays out of the physical balance.

64
00:12:19,834 --> 00:12:33,916
Allocation runs every day for operations, often with provisional data. Monthly closing uses final data, and the monthly factor comes from monthly quantities, not from averaging the daily factors.

65
00:12:34,416 --> 00:12:45,444
The workflow runs from import and validation, through preliminary estimates, conversions, balance and allocation, to checks, exception review, approval and publication.

66
00:12:45,944 --> 00:12:57,647
Everything is retained: data versions, period, method, parameters, user, timestamp and reasons for corrections. The same inputs and rules must always reproduce the same result.

67
00:12:58,147 --> 00:13:07,620
The final report gives each well its oil, gas and water, runtime, factors and quality status, and from them the water cut and gas–oil ratio.

68
00:13:08,120 --> 00:13:17,375
Commercial entitlement is calculated afterwards, under the contracts. Physical production shares and revenue shares are not assumed to be the same.

69
00:13:19,275 --> 00:13:31,942
Back allocation does not replace reliable meters or good well tests. Weak preliminary estimates mean greater uncertainty, even when the balance closes perfectly.

70
00:13:32,442 --> 00:13:46,781
Back allocation connects shared production accounting to the sources of that production. Measured totals, well data, operating time and material balances are combined to determine a reconciled contribution from each well.
